  "narrative": "The new discovery batch primarily tests robustness and specificity of the large accepted associations, plus several focused follow-ups. Using the same working convention that about 0.025 absolute dependency-score difference corresponds to a roughly 10% relative difference on the observed negative-scale dependency outcomes, the robustness results are decisive. For SMARCA2 dependency, SMARCA4 loss is associated with much stronger dependency both in Avana screens, H23/R14 estimate +0.781 (95% CI +0.760 to +0.802), and in non-Avana screens, H24/R15 estimate +0.801 (95% CI +0.748 to +0.853). These are far above the threshold and exclude null in the hypothesis direction, so I accept these stratum-specific robustness claims as supporting the global H5 association without suggesting it is confined to one CRISPR library. For WRN dependency, MSI-high is associated with much stronger dependency in Avana screens, H25/R16 +0.794 (95% CI +0.764 to +0.825), and in higher-Cas9-activity screens, H43/R17 +0.794 (95% CI +0.757 to +0.831), supporting H7. For the reversed NFE2L2 claim, KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency in both Avana screens, H26/R18 +0.796 (95% CI +0.774 to +0.818), and non-Avana screens, H27/R19 +0.794 (95% CI +0.746 to +0.842), supporting H20. I closed these individual robustness claims because the library/QC checks are sufficiently informative for the strata tested, while the global claims remain active for broader interpretation. H28 was deferred because the non-Avana MSI-high cell is expected to be too small for a valid discovery comparison.\n\nH39 tests ARID1A loss among SMARCA4-intact cells. The signed estimate is essentially null, -0.003 (95% CI -0.025 to +0.019), opposite direction and below the threshold. I reject the registered ARID1A-specific claim. This does not prove biological specificity, but it is consistent with the global ARID1A null and supports the interpretation that the very large SMARCA2 association is primarily aligned with SMARCA4 loss rather than broader chromatin-remodeling losses in this dataset.\n\nThe driver/co-mutation follow-ups are more inconclusive. H21 tests whether EGFR-amplification effects on EGFR dependency are stronger in adenocarcinoma than squamous histology. The interaction estimate is small and in the opposite signed direction, -0.018 (95% CI -0.077 to +0.040), and the interval includes null and subthreshold effects in both directions. I leave it unresolved and active because direct subgroup estimates H29/H30 remain available and could clarify whether any overall weak signal concentrates by histology.\n\nH48 tests KRAS dependency in STK11-loss cells. The signed estimate is +0.031 (95% CI -0.010 to +0.073), above the approximate 10% threshold at the point estimate, but the interval crosses zero and includes effects below threshold. This is biologically plausible co-mutation context but not conclusive. I leave H48 unresolved and active, and I request independent validation because validation is most useful here: the discovery point is promising but statistically and practically uncertain, whereas the largest accepted effects do not require validation before being recognized as dataset associations.\n\nH17 is the strongest new mechanistic result. PTEN loss is associated with substantially stronger STAT3 dependency: signed estimate +0.267 (95% CI +0.215 to +0.319). In raw terms, PTEN-loss cells have a more negative STAT3 dependency score by about 0.215 to 0.319 units, far exceeding the approximate 10% threshold. I accept H17 as a dataset association, but not as a causal or externally generalizable claim. I mark it active and add robustness proposals because a large new association should be checked for technical or compositional confounding before overinterpreting.\n\nH13 tests MYC amplification with POLR2A dependency. The estimate is tiny, +0.004 (95% CI -0.017 to +0.025), with the interval mostly below or at the threshold and crossing null. I reject the overall association, while the high-growth-rate refinement H49 can still address context-specific hypotheses. H18 tests whether KRAS mutation is associated with weaker RIT1 dependency. The estimate is negligible, +0.004 (95% CI -0.015 to +0.022), so I reject it. Overall, the new evidence strengthens robustness of the three accepted large effects, adds a large new PTEN/STAT3 dataset association, and identifies KRAS/STK11 as the main borderline claim worth independent validation.",
  "research_notes": "Current accepted dataset associations: H5 SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency; H7 MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency; H20 KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency; H17 PTEN loss -> stronger STAT3 dependency. Robustness now closed/accepted: H23/H24 library-stratified SMARCA2; H25/H43 Avana/high-Cas9 WRN; H26/H27 library-stratified NFE2L2. H39 null among SMARCA4-intact cells supports SMARCA4 specificity but is not proof. Unresolved: H2 EGFR overall after weak validation; H4 KRAS overall; H21 EGFR lineage interaction; H48 KRAS dependency in STK11-loss cells pending requested validation; H11/H12/H14/H19/H22/H28-H56 mostly untested or pending. H28 deferred due likely small non-Avana MSI-high cells. Priorities: (1) assess H48 validation when it arrives; (2) run STAT3 PTEN-loss robustness proposals by library/Cas9/lineage; (3) test direct EGFR and KRAS histology subgroups H29-H32 and ERBB2 subtype checks; (4) selectively test MYC high-growth refinements H49/H50; (5) keep neutral-marker TMED10 screens lower priority unless needed for false-positive background. All claims remain associations in supplied dataset, not causal or generalizable mechanisms unless independently validated.",
